From: l4h <l4h@localhost>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:53:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] x86_64: add an IRQControl kexec handoff

Provide seL4_X86_Kexec (the IRQControl Kexec invocation) on core zero.
Root supplies a physical control page, identity page tables, parking code
and a trampoline, after staging the image and quiescing devices. The
kernel validates scalar fields before stopping any CPUs; image parsing,
copy planning, DMA shutdown and APIC-variant checks remain root's job.

Park APs through the remote-call IPI so CPUs waiting on the big kernel
lock also respond. Acknowledge from the parking page under the new CR3,
not from the old kernel's IPI barrier. Preserve the kernel mapping only
for the CR3 transition, flush global/PCID translations, mask LAPIC timers,
drain in-service interrupts and mask every IOAPIC pin before jumping.
Keep pic_disable in runtime text because kexec runs after boot memory is
reclaimed.

Supply relocation-free libsel4 blobs for root to copy into its control
pages. The trampoline copies or zero-fills segments on its own stack, loads a
private GDT, leaves long mode and enters the new kernel with the multiboot2
registers. Clear PCIDE before PG, then LME and PAE. Set PSE and clear
SMEP/SMAP for seL4's user-mapped 2 MiB boot pages, as GRUB leaves them.
APs stay in a stackless halt loop until the next kernel starts them with
INIT-SIPI-SIPI.

Preserve the firmware memory map in root's extra bootinfo on multiboot2
boots, and mark a map truncated on either boot protocol. Root needs the
complete firmware map, not its own free-memory view, to build the next MBI.

This grants the IRQControl holder arbitrary privileged execution. It is
an unverified x86_64 extension; the caller must keep all control memory,
page tables and sources outside every destination.
